Subject: =?UTF-8?q?=F0=9F=94=97=20Ask-only=20links:=20a=20linked=20machine?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20may=20ask=20opted-in=20threads=20and=20read=20the=20answers?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Spec: fleet docs/t3-ask-link-spec.md. Upstream proposal:
pingdotgg/t3code discussion #40978.

Behaviours:
- New link access level "ask", below read-only: CLI
  `t3 environment link --access ask`, the link dialog's picker ("Ask only"),
  the link-request card on web and mobile, and every schema that lists link
  access levels. An ask session holds only the new scope orchestration:ask,
  so nothing guarded by orchestration:read opens to it; approving one needs
  orchestration:read on the pairing code or browser session. Approval codes
  get the ASK- prefix. The level is for linked environments only: outside-app
  pickers hide it and approval refuses it for other clients. A machine
  advertises the capability askLinks; linking at ask to a machine without it
  is refused before any request.
- Per-thread opt-in "Answer linked questions" in the thread menu (web header
  and sidebar menus, mobile thread row), off by default, off on forks. It is
  the command thread.linked-questions.set, which needs orchestration:operate
  and access:write and has no MCP tool, palette entry or keybinding. While it
  is on, the chat header shows "Answers linked questions".
- An ask session may call three tools and nothing else: t3_ask_list (threads
  that opted in: id, title, machine label), t3_ask_send (one plain-text
  question, at most 8 000 characters) and t3_ask_wait (the answer text of the
  turn that question started, at most 20 000 characters, only for the asking
  link's own question). Every other tool refuses it in the shared access
  declarations (McpToolAccess), tool and event discovery hides everything
  else (MCP milestone events included), the three link thread routes answer
  403 ask_link, and the asking machine refuses other tools on an ask link
  before anything leaves it. A link of any level may use the three tools.
  Errors use a fixed vocabulary; a thread that does not answer and a missing
  thread get the same reply.
- The answering turn runs without tools whatever the thread's mode is. The
  question's run is marked toolless; turn start passes tools: "none" for that
  turn only, so later turns keep the thread's own mode. Claude answers with an
  empty tool list, no MCP servers (strict config), denied permission
  callback, verbatim prompts and slash commands off, and needs Claude Code
  2.1.248 or later; tool calls the model writes are neither run nor shown. A
  provider without tools.supportsToollessTurns (every other one today)
  refuses the question before a session opens. A question is refused when the
  thread's workspace is missing; nothing is restored for it.
- A question is inert text: no slash or local command handling, no mention or
  context expansion, no attachments, fresh context, output schema or model
  override. The provider prompt starts with a sender note that names the
  asking machine and says the thread may decline.
- A question always runs as its own turn: it queues behind a running turn and
  never steers or restarts one, and no other message, promoted queued
  message, agent send or delegated-task notice joins a question's turn (the
  note about delegated tasks a Stop interrupted waits for the next ordinary
  turn). A queued question cannot be edited or promoted. With an active
  native goal the answering turn ends after its answer.
- The question shows in the thread as "From <machine>", sent and queued, on
  web and mobile.
- Limits: 10 questions per 10 minutes per link (in memory) and one open
  question per thread across all links (read from stored runs); both answer
  the retryable code rate_limited.
- Usage limits: a question that the provider stops on a usage limit fails
  with "The thread hit its usage limit before answering. Ask again later."
  and is an ordinary failure for the thread, so the usage-limit queue hold,
  recovery, Resume and banner do not apply to it. A question is refused while
  an ordinary run's usage limit holds the thread.
- New server setting linkedEnvironmentsInboundMaxAccess (default
  full-access), set in Settings → Environments → List ("Links into this
  machine") and lockable through the managed policy; changing it needs
  access:write. A link above it is refused at approval (before the pairing
  code is spent) and at token exchange, and an existing link above it is
  refused and revoked on its next use at every entry a link token has (/mcp,
  the link thread routes including open streams, client pairing). A link that
  was above the cap stays refused after the cap is raised and after a
  restart: the server keeps, in its secret store, when each level was last
  above the cap. A history that cannot be read counts every level above ask
  as above the cap at that start.
- The environments map and its access meter draw six levels; the five old
  levels look as before.
- User docs (docs/user/remote-access.md) and internal docs (environment-auth,
  remote, adding-a-provider) describe the level, the opt-in, the cap and
  tools.supportsToollessTurns. New catalog strings are English in every
  locale.

Not covered: the mobile settings screen has no row for the cap; t3_ask_list
and t3_ask_wait have no rate limit of their own; the evals cases added under
evals/mcp-tool-use were not run against a model.

Port to 21b52875ea: upstream e414cfd4be (#38882, durable workflows) adds the workflowsEnabled setting next to linkedEnvironmentsInboundMaxAccess in settings.ts (both kept), the t3_workflow tool, workflow milestone event types and a Workflows mock in the MCP tool test layer. t3_workflow is declared through McpToolAccess.toLayer, so the default deny refuses it to an ask link, and layerAskLinkVisibility hides it and the workflow event types from ask discovery. McpToolAccess.testkit.ts's toolHandlerServices gains upstream's Workflows mock, AgentContextBudget.test.ts keeps using it, mcpToolListJsonBytes is 62922 (upstream's 61724 plus the same 1198 bytes the ask tools added before), MilestoneEvents.test.ts serves upstream's workflow settings with linked environments on and keeps the host-token and ask-token cases, and ThreadManagementService.test.ts keeps upstream's workflowRun provenance assertion beside the forged ask question.

