+- An **Ask only** link cannot read threads.
+
+### Let linked machines ask a thread
+
+A thread answers questions from linked machines only after you turn on **Answer
+linked questions** in its thread menu. It is off by default. Only a session that
+manages access can change it; agents, links and scheduled tasks cannot. While it
+is on, the thread's header shows **Answers linked questions**.
+
+- Agents on a linked machine list these threads with `t3_ask_list`, ask one with
+  `t3_ask_send` and read the reply with `t3_ask_wait`. A link at any level can
+  ask; an **Ask only** link can do nothing else.
+- A question shows in the thread marked **From** and the asking machine's name.
+  The thread answers in text with every tool off for that answer: no shell,
+  files, MCP servers, browser, web or subagents. It may decline. Its next normal
+  turn keeps its own mode.
+- Only threads running on Claude can answer; on other providers the question is
+  refused. A thread whose worktree is gone refuses it too.
+- Each link may ask 10 questions in 10 minutes, and a thread takes one question
+  at a time.
+- A question that stops on the thread's usage limit fails and is not resumed at
+  the reset, even with auto-resume on. A thread waiting at its usage limit
+  refuses questions. Ask again later.
+- Turning the setting off refuses questions still waiting in the queue.
+
+### Limit links into a machine
+
+**Links into this machine** in **Settings → Environments → Linked environments**
+(the **List** view) sets the broadest access another machine may link here with.
+It defaults to Full access. A pairing above it fails and names the limit. A link
+already above it stops working and stays stopped even if you raise the limit
+later; link it again within the limit. A [managed policy](./managed-policy.md#settings)
+can set and lock it with the key `linkedEnvironmentsInboundMaxAccess`, for
+example `{ "value": "ask", "locked": true }`.
 
